    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The single decision that matters is oil-based versus water-based, and it comes down to one honest trade-off: warmth and durability against clarity and non-yellowing. Everything else is technique.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <h2 id="oil-vs-water" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          1. Oil vs Water-Based: The Only Decision That Really Matters
        </h2>

        <p>
          Nearly every other choice in furniture finishing is reversible or minor. This one is neither, because it determines the colour of the piece for the rest of its life.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oil-Based Polyurethane</h3>

        <p>
          Oil-based poly cures by reacting with oxygen, building a hard, solvent-resistant film. It goes on with a distinct amber cast, and — this is the important part — it keeps ambering. A piece finished in oil-based poly is noticeably warmer after a year and warmer still after five.
        </p>

        <p>
          On walnut, cherry, oak and mahogany that progression is not a defect; it is most of the reason people choose it. The finish deepens the figure, warms the tone and makes the wood look older and richer than a clear finish would. On white maple, ash, birch or anything painted, the same behaviour turns a crisp piece yellow and there is no undoing it.
        </p>

        <p>
          It also has a long open time, which makes brushing forgiving, and it builds quickly — three coats is genuine protection. Against that: strong solvent odour that lingers for days, longer waits between coats, and cleanup in mineral spirits.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water-Based Polyurethane</h3>

        <p>
          Water-based poly carries the resin in water, so it dries clear, stays clear, and smells of very little. Over a painted dresser or a maple table it looks like nothing at all, which is exactly the point — you see the wood or the paint, not the finish.
        </p>

        <p>
          The old objection was durability, and on modern formulations that objection is largely obsolete. A good water-based furniture finish resists water rings and heat about as well as an oil-based one. Where it genuinely differs is open time: it sets fast, so on a large surface you have to work briskly and keep a wet edge.
        </p>

        <p>
          It also raises the grain on the first coat, because water swells wood fibres. That is easily handled — sand lightly after the first coat and it will not happen again — but it surprises people who have only used oil-based finishes.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Practical Rule</h3>

        <p>
          Choose by the wood and the intent. <strong>Warm-toned hardwood where you want depth and age: oil-based.</strong> <strong>Pale wood, painted work, or anything where the colour must stay exactly as it is now: water-based.</strong> Everything else — smell, cleanup, dry time — follows from that choice rather than driving it.
        </p>

        <h2 id="sheen" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          2. Choosing a Sheen
        </h2>

        <p>
          Sheen is not in any sense a measure of protection. All the sheens within a product line share the same resin and offer the same durability; the difference is a flattening agent that scatters light. Choose it on appearance alone.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Gloss</strong> reflects most and gives the greatest sense of depth, which suits formal pieces and dark woods. It also shows every dust nib, every brush mark, every fingerprint and every scratch. It is the least forgiving sheen both to apply and to live with.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Semi-gloss</strong> keeps some of that depth while hiding considerably more. A reasonable compromise on a dining table where you want a bit of reflection.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Satin</strong> is what most furniture should be finished in, and it is what most commercially made furniture uses. It reads as wood rather than as a coating, and it conceals dust, minor flaws and daily handling marks remarkably well.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Matte</strong> reflects almost nothing, which suits rustic pieces and painted work but can look slightly dead on figured hardwood where you wanted the grain to shine.
        </p>

        <p>
          One technical note that catches people out: the flattening agent settles. A can of satin that has not been stirred thoroughly will apply as gloss at the start and matte at the end. Stir slowly and completely before use, and stir again partway through a large job — but never shake, because every bubble you whip in ends up in the film.
        </p>


        <!-- Product Reviews -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Polyurethanes for Furniture, Reviewed
          </h2>

