                            {con}
                          </li>
                        ))}
                      </ul>
                    </div>
                  </div>

                  <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-xl p-4 border border-gray-100">
                    <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-1">The Bottom Line</p>
                    <p class="text-gray-900 font-bold">{product.bottomLine}</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h2 id="by-piece" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          4. Which Finish for Which Piece
        </h2>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dining Tables</h3>
        <p>
          The hardest-working furniture surface in the house: hot plates, wet glasses, spills, and constant wiping. Use four to five coats of a tough finish, oil-based if the wood is warm-toned and a premium water-based if it is not. Satin or semi-gloss. This is the one piece where the extra coats are genuinely worth the extra day.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Coffee and Side Tables</h3>
        <p>
          Similar exposure to a dining table but less heat. Three to four coats is ample. Watch for plant pots, which are the most common cause of water damage on a coffee table — a saucer and a felt pad prevent a repair.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bookshelves and Cabinets</h3>
        <p>
          Very light duty — these surfaces are barely touched once loaded. Two or three coats of a water-based finish is plenty, and the fast recoat time means the whole job fits in a day. There is no reason to spend on a premium finish here.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Chairs, Stools and Turned Legs</h3>
        <p>
          Shape is the problem, not durability. Wipe-on poly is the right answer for anything with spindles, carving or mouldings, because a brush inevitably floods the inside corners and leaves runs that you will only notice once they have cured.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Painted Furniture</h3>
        <p>
          Water-based only, without exception. An oil-based topcoat over white or pale paint will turn it cream within a year and yellow within three. This is the single most common finishing regret we hear about, and it is entirely avoidable.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Antiques and Restoration</h3>
        <p>
          Think carefully before putting polyurethane on an old piece at all. Poly is difficult to repair locally and difficult to remove, and on a period piece it can reduce value considerably. Shellac and traditional varnishes are repairable in ways polyurethane is not.
        </p>

        <h2 id="applying" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          5. Getting a Glass-Smooth Finish
        </h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Sand properly first, and stop at the right grit.</strong> Work up to 180 or 220 for most furniture. Going finer burnishes the surface and can prevent stain and finish from keying in evenly, which shows up as blotching.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Remove every trace of dust.</strong> Vacuum, then wipe with a cloth barely damp with the appropriate solvent — water for water-based finishes, mineral spirits for oil. Dust in the first coat is the defect people notice most.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Stir, never shake.</strong> Shaking whips air into the finish and every bubble transfers to the surface. Stir slowly with a flat stick, lifting the settled flattening agent off the bottom.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Thin coats, always.</strong> A thick coat sags, traps solvent and cures soft. Three thin coats are stronger, clearer and smoother than two heavy ones, and they dry faster in total.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Use the right applicator.</strong> A quality synthetic brush for water-based finishes — natural bristle absorbs water and goes limp. A natural bristle brush or a foam applicator for oil-based. Our guide to <a href="/best-brush-for-water-based-polyurethane/">brushes for water-based polyurethane</a> covers the specifics.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Keep a wet edge and do not overwork it.</strong> Lay the finish on, tip it off once in the direction of the grain, and move on. Going back over a surface that has begun to set is what produces drag marks and a rough texture.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Sand lightly between coats.</strong> 320 or 400 grit, two or three light passes, just enough to remove nibs and raised grain. Wipe the dust off before recoating.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Leave the last coat alone</strong>, unless you intend to rub out the entire surface with fine abrasive and wax — which is how a genuinely glass-smooth top is achieved, and which requires enough film thickness to sand into safely.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Temperature and Humidity</h3>

        <p>
          Both finishes are fussier about conditions than the tins admit. Below about 60°F an oil-based polyurethane cures slowly and can stay tacky for days; a water-based one may not coalesce properly at all, leaving a film that stays soft and cloudy no matter how long you wait. An unheated garage or a cold basement workshop in autumn is by far the most common place where this goes wrong.
        </p>

        <p>
          High humidity slows water-based finishes considerably, since the water has nowhere to go. If the air is damp, extend your recoat intervals rather than trusting the touch test — a surface can feel dry while the film underneath is still full of water, and recoating over that traps it permanently.
        </p>

        <p>
          The comfortable working range for both is roughly 65 to 75°F with moderate humidity, which for most people means finishing indoors rather than in a workshop. It is worth moving a piece into the house for finishing and moving it back out afterwards.
        </p>

        <h2 id="cure" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          6. Cure Time and Real Durability
        </h2>

        <p>
          The distinction between dry and cured causes more damage to newly finished furniture than any application error. Dry means the solvent or water has left and the surface is firm. Cured means the film has reached its final hardness, and that takes far longer.
        </p>

        <p>
          Water-based polyurethane is typically handleable in a few hours and recoatable in two to three, but full hardness takes one to two weeks. Oil-based is touch-dry in six to eight hours, recoatable overnight, and fully cured in three to four weeks.
        </p>

        <p>
          During that window the film is soft enough to take an impression. A lamp base, a laptop, a fruit bowl or a plastic placemat left in one spot will emboss the surface permanently, and a felt pad left on a fresh finish can bond to it. This is why a table that looked perfect on day three has a visible ring by week two.
        </p>

        <p>
          Practical guidance: light use after 48 hours, nothing left standing in one place for two weeks, and no placemats or tablecloths for a month. If you must use the piece sooner, put something under objects that lifts them clear and move them daily.
        </p>

        <p>
          One last point on longevity. A polyurethane film fails at the edges first — the front lip of a table, the arms of a chair, the leading edge of a shelf — because that is where it gets handled and knocked. Applying an extra coat to those areas specifically costs almost nothing and noticeably extends the life of the finish.
        </p>


        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">Common Mistakes</h2>
          <div class="space-y-4">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Using floor-grade polyurethane on furniture</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Floor finishes are formulated for abrasion resistance and build, not for clarity or feel. On a table top they go on thick, look plastic, and are far harder to rub out to a fine surface.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Shaking the can</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Every bubble you introduce ends up in the film. Stir slowly with a stick instead, and never shake — this single habit causes more ruined table tops than any product defect.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Recoating before the previous coat has hardened</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Touch-dry is not ready. Oil-based poly wants overnight between coats and water-based at least two to three hours. Recoating early traps solvent and leaves a soft finish that never fully hardens.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Sanding the final coat to fix a flaw</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Sanding through the last coat means recoating the whole surface, not the spot. Fix defects between coats, and leave the final coat alone unless you are deliberately rubbing out the entire panel.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border-l-4 border-red-500 rounded-r-xl p-6">
              <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Putting anything heavy on it at one week</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Dry to the touch in hours, fully cured in weeks. A lamp base or a laptop left on a two-week-old table top will emboss the finish permanently.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Frequently Asked Questions
        </h2>

        <div class="space-y-6">
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best polyurethane for furniture?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">For most furniture, a water-based polyurethane — it stays clear, does not yellow over time, and has almost no odour. Choose oil-based when you want the warm amber tone it adds to walnut, cherry and oak, or when the piece is a hard-working table top that needs maximum film toughness.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Does water-based polyurethane yellow?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">No, and that is its main advantage. It dries genuinely clear and stays that way for decades. Oil-based polyurethane ambers noticeably within the first year and keeps deepening, which looks wonderful on walnut and quite wrong on white maple or a painted piece.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How many coats of polyurethane on furniture?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Three coats is the sensible default for most pieces. A dining table or anything that will see drinks and heat benefits from four or five. More than that adds thickness without meaningful protection and starts to look like a coating rather than a finish.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use wipe-on or brush-on polyurethane?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Wipe-on for anything with detail, carving or turned legs, and for beginners generally — it is almost impossible to get runs or brush marks. Brush-on builds much faster, so it is the better choice on large flat surfaces like a table top where you want film thickness.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put polyurethane over stain?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes, provided the stain is fully dry — typically 24 hours for oil-based stain, longer in humid conditions. The one combination to be careful with is water-based poly over oil-based stain, which needs the stain properly cured or the finish can lift.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">What sheen is best for furniture?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Satin for most pieces. It looks like wood rather than plastic, and it hides fingerprints, dust and small surface flaws far better than gloss. Use semi-gloss or gloss where you specifically want depth and reflection, and matte on rustic or painted pieces.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">How long before furniture can be used?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Light use after 24 to 48 hours, but full cure takes seven to thirty days depending on the product. Until then the film is soft enough to emboss, so avoid placemats, laptops, plant pots and anything left in one place for long.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="border border-gray-200 rounded-xl p-6">
            <h3 class="font-black text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to sand between coats?</h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Yes, lightly. A quick pass with 320 or 400 grit knocks down dust nibs and raised grain and gives the next coat something to key into. It is not about removing material — two or three light strokes per area is enough.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
          Related Wood Finishing Guides
