        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 rounded-2xl p-8 text-gray-200">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="mb-4">
            For the majority of furniture, a water-based polyurethane in satin is the right answer: it stays clear, it does not yellow, it smells of very little, and three coats gives a finish that looks like wood rather than like a coating.
          </p>
          <p class="m-0">
            Reach for oil-based when the warmth is the point — walnut, cherry, oak — or when the piece is a hard-used table top and you want the toughest film available. And whichever you choose, the technique matters more than the brand: thin coats, no shaking, and real patience between them.
          </p>
        </div>

